WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.470
-Our next guest is a talented
actor you've seen in

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:04.870
"The Deuce" and "Ocean's Eight."

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.206
He stars in "The Many Saints
of Newark," which is in theaters

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:09.041
and begins streaming on
HBO Max October 1st.

00:00:09.041 --> 00:00:12.445
Let's take a look.
-♪ When will I...♪

00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:17.383
-[ Sighs ]

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:19.986
-Hey, Sil, I need to talk to
my Uncle Dickie.

00:00:20.719 --> 00:00:22.755
-He's not here.

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:24.757
-His car's here.

00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:26.559
-Battery's dead.

00:00:26.892 --> 00:00:30.663
-You know where he is?

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:32.765
-Who am I? Rand McNally?

00:00:32.765 --> 00:00:35.334
-Please welcome to the show
Michael Gandolfini.

00:00:35.334 --> 00:00:37.337
♪♪

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:40.073
[ Applause ]

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:41.541
-Thanks so much
for being here, man.

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:42.908
I really appreciate it.
Come on up.

00:00:42.908 --> 00:00:45.078
-Of course, thank you.

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:50.149
-Thanks for being here, Michael,
and congrats on the film.

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:51.684
I really enjoyed it.

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:55.354
I imagine, for any young actor,
it would be daunting to take on

00:00:55.354 --> 00:00:57.757
the role of young Tony Soprano.

00:00:57.757 --> 00:01:01.294
Plus, of course, you have
the element of being, you know,

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:03.429
your father's part
that he made famous.

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:06.899
-What was your -- What was
the process of going through

00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:08.601
to decide you wanted to
take this on?

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:12.639
-Yeah, well, one of
the things that I think

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:15.474
was equally terrifying
and really helped was that

00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:17.543
I had to audition
like three times.

00:01:17.543 --> 00:01:20.813
So it was like
three months of, like --

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:22.949
first being like,
"I don't know.

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:25.819
You know, I don't know Tony.
I'd never seen the show.

00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:28.421
I don't --
I want to be my own actor.

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:29.789
I don't really know if
I can do it."

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:33.492
And then, as the auditions
went on, I just sort of thought,

00:01:33.492 --> 00:01:35.895
"You know,
you got to do a good audition

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:37.830
and get in front of
good people."

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.566
And I started to, like, fall in
love with Tony more and more.

00:01:40.566 --> 00:01:43.235
So by the end, when it was like,
"We're going to do it,"

00:01:43.235 --> 00:01:44.670
I loved Tony.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:45.971
-That's really great.
-Yeah.

00:01:45.971 --> 00:01:48.707
-Now, this is -- You mentioned
not seeing the show.

00:01:48.707 --> 00:01:50.643
You were really young when
the show started.

00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:52.078
-Mm-hmm.

00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:54.280
-And, of course, your dad is
a completely different person

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.681
than Tony Soprano.

00:01:55.681 --> 00:01:58.351
Did you -- Were you ever around
when he was rehearsing?

00:01:58.351 --> 00:02:00.586
Was that something
you remember as a kid at all?

00:02:00.586 --> 00:02:02.755
-I mean, not really.

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:05.625
I -- He was just my dad,
you know?

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:09.028
And he -- I mean,
he actually had this --

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:11.664
He had, like -- In his
apartment, he had this bookcase.

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:13.832
And he would, like --
It was, like, a secret door.

00:02:13.832 --> 00:02:16.368
And you can, like, open up
this secret bookcase door,

00:02:16.368 --> 00:02:19.138
and he'd go into his little
cave and he'd close it.

00:02:19.138 --> 00:02:20.573
And it was, like, soundproof.

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:23.343
So, like, that's -- I remember
him going in there to, like,

00:02:23.343 --> 00:02:26.146
run lines, and, like, him and
his coach would go in there.

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:30.049
But, you know, I never --
I ran around set.

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:33.185
I got fake black eyes
by the makeup crew.

00:02:33.185 --> 00:02:35.621
And, you know, we'd be like,
"Dad, look."

00:02:35.621 --> 00:02:39.124
But besides that,
I didn't really know anything.

00:02:39.124 --> 00:02:42.762
-So, these are all, you know,
characters, obviously,

00:02:42.762 --> 00:02:44.397
from the from "The Sopranos,"
but none of the same actors.

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:45.832
So none of the people
you've met over your life,

00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:47.366
save for, you know, David Chase.

00:02:47.366 --> 00:02:48.801
Did he --
-Yeah.

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:51.070
-Did you have a level of comfort
being with somebody like that,

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.739
who you've known, obviously,
your whole life?

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:56.909
-I didn't know David
personally too well.

00:02:56.909 --> 00:03:00.846
I'd known him, but there's
a level of comfort as an actor

00:03:00.846 --> 00:03:04.617
when you go -- I mean,
I'm walking into your world.

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:07.619
I mean, he would come up
and run and be like,

00:03:07.619 --> 00:03:08.921
"You know, say this."

00:03:08.921 --> 00:03:12.791
And, like, it'd be this amazing,
beautiful, funny line

00:03:12.791 --> 00:03:14.927
that he would just
kind of come up with.

00:03:14.927 --> 00:03:18.264
So it was a real comfort
to be able to go --

00:03:18.264 --> 00:03:20.032
I'd call it, like
"The David thumbs-up."

00:03:20.032 --> 00:03:23.736
Like, after every scene,
I'd be like...

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:24.937
"What do you think?"

00:03:24.937 --> 00:03:26.973
And he would just go --
give me a thumbs-up,

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:29.775
and I'd be happy that
we got the scene.

00:03:29.775 --> 00:03:32.611
-You are still a student at NYU.
-I am.

00:03:32.611 --> 00:03:35.014
-You got to --
You made your own major.

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:37.850
Is this something that NYU kids
are doing these days?

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:39.785
-Yeah.
So, you can go to Gallatin,

00:03:39.785 --> 00:03:41.687
and you create your major.

00:03:41.687 --> 00:03:44.890
So, I mean, people major
in amazing things,

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:49.862
like, you know, dance mixed
with, you know, politics,

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:51.831
and they'd do some
incredible performance.

00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:54.700
And mine's like,
I just go for character study.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.669
That's how I formed my major.

00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:58.604
-But character study --
does that --

00:03:58.604 --> 00:04:00.372
that's not just acting, right?

00:04:00.372 --> 00:04:02.175
-I actually have never --

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:04.177
I went to Tisch for
a semester and I left,

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:07.179
so I've never taken an
acting class at NYU since.

00:04:07.179 --> 00:04:09.281
But it's a lot of, like --

00:04:09.281 --> 00:04:12.218
I took, like, a
consumerism class to learn about

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:14.754
spending patterns in America.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:17.724
And then I've taken
filmmaking and directing.

00:04:17.724 --> 00:04:20.926
I took a class about
the history of penicillin

00:04:20.926 --> 00:04:23.496
for my for my science credit.

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:27.766
So anything that has to do with
sort of people, you know?

00:04:27.766 --> 00:04:33.406
Psychology, all those sort of
classes in that world.

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:35.107
-You probably
didn't need much help

00:04:35.107 --> 00:04:36.576
as far as acting classes went.

00:04:36.576 --> 00:04:38.778
As a young man, I mean,
I think we all remember

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:40.213
how you crushed Prince Charming.

00:04:40.213 --> 00:04:42.981
I mean, just --
-Look at that hair, too.

00:04:42.981 --> 00:04:44.983
-I mean, the hair is great,
but I think that

00:04:44.983 --> 00:04:49.221
not enough people, when they're
watching people play Charming,

00:04:49.221 --> 00:04:52.892
credit the look he gives
when he's staring at the shoe.

00:04:52.892 --> 00:04:54.260
-At the shoe.
-Yeah.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:55.628
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:55.628 --> 00:04:57.129
-This Prince Charming might not
be looking for Cinderella

00:04:57.129 --> 00:04:59.732
as much as, like, he might
have a thing about feet.

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:01.867
-You know, "Into the Woods"
is a darker

00:05:01.867 --> 00:05:04.537
sort of adaptation
of this fairy tale,

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:08.607
so maybe he had some sort of
foot fetish, you know?

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:12.010
-Your first day on set for
"Many Saints" --

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.013
which, I guess, this does seem
like a rite of passage

00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:17.617
for anybody entering the sort of
"Sopranos" cinematic universe --

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:19.218
got slapped around a little bit.

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:23.622
-Oh, yeah, the scene was --
It didn't end up in the movie,

00:05:23.622 --> 00:05:27.259
but Alessandro sl--
Dickie slaps Tony,

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:29.028
and Tony was like,
"Why did you do that?"

00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:31.997
And then they talk a little,

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:33.933
and then Dickie slaps
Tony again.

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:35.568
Tony slaps Dickie.

00:05:35.568 --> 00:05:36.868
They fight.

00:05:36.868 --> 00:05:38.237
Johnny breaks them up.

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:40.973
And then Johnny slaps Tony.

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:44.009
-Now, when you get slapped
that many times,

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:47.212
how do you feel when
you're told the scene got cut?

00:05:47.212 --> 00:05:48.714
-Yeah, well, it was --

00:05:48.714 --> 00:05:51.483
I was pretty shocked.

00:05:51.483 --> 00:05:55.521
I mean, it was -- We also only
shot that scene that day.

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:59.892
So it was like 12 hours
of three slaps a scene.

00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:02.595
And Bernthal was like bra--

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:04.597
He would be like,
"You know, I'm a pro,

00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:07.132
so, like, my slaps
don't hurt, right, Mikey?"

00:06:07.132 --> 00:06:09.701
And you don't look at
The Punisher and go like,

00:06:09.701 --> 00:06:11.737
"Eh, these slaps don't hurt
at all, man.

00:06:11.737 --> 00:06:13.439
Like, I'm totally game."

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:16.275
And he's, like,
just hitting me hard.

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:18.610
Yeah, but it was --
It was great, you know?

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:20.113
It was, like,
the rite of passage,

00:06:20.113 --> 00:06:21.914
like you said, and it was great.

00:06:21.914 --> 00:06:23.882
-You also -- I guess,
was it your first film role

00:06:23.882 --> 00:06:25.184
was "Ocean's Eight"?

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:26.685
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:06:26.685 --> 00:06:29.355
-I mean, I think people to this
day still say it's your movie.

00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:32.491
-Yeah, exactly. It's my -- Yeah.

00:06:32.491 --> 00:06:34.694
-I mean, a lot of
Oscar winners are in it.

00:06:34.694 --> 00:06:36.095
-Yeah.
-An incredibly talented cast.

00:06:36.095 --> 00:06:37.263
-It was amazing.

00:06:37.263 --> 00:06:38.664
I mean, it was one of those --

00:06:38.664 --> 00:06:41.200
You know, I played
Busboy Number 2.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.534
-Yeah, of course. We all know.

00:06:42.534 --> 00:06:44.670
-A privilege. Exactly.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:47.172
And it was just
one of those things.

00:06:47.172 --> 00:06:50.375
You get to go to
a giant set and sound stage

00:06:50.375 --> 00:06:53.512
and think like, "I cannot
believe I'm here in the tux."

00:06:53.512 --> 00:06:56.748
But that scene
took four days to film.

00:06:56.748 --> 00:06:59.585
I have like four lines in it,
and it took four days.

00:06:59.585 --> 00:07:02.255
The first day,
they didn't get to it

00:07:02.255 --> 00:07:03.989
because they were
running behind, whatever.

00:07:03.989 --> 00:07:07.326
The second day, a snowstorm hit,
so we had to --

00:07:07.326 --> 00:07:09.462
We had to go to set
and drive back.

00:07:09.462 --> 00:07:12.264
And then, the third day,
something happened again.

00:07:12.264 --> 00:07:14.967
And, finally, the fourth day,
I got to deliver.

00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:17.169
But I was, like, outside,
like, really, like,

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:19.071
rehearsing these like
four lines.

00:07:19.071 --> 00:07:21.607
Like, I was really,
like, working --

00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:23.909
-That's a nervous --
I mean, nobody, I think,

00:07:23.909 --> 00:07:25.944
would not be nervous amongst
that group of people.

00:07:25.944 --> 00:07:27.846
-Yeah. But it was amazing.

00:07:27.846 --> 00:07:29.615
-You're amazing in this.
Thanks so much for being here.

00:07:29.615 --> 00:07:31.050
Congratulations so much
on the film.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.218
Thanks for making time for us.
-Thank you.

00:07:32.218 --> 00:07:33.753
-"The Many Saints of Newark"

00:07:33.753 --> 00:07:35.721
is in theaters
and on HBO Max October 1st.

